HEML a superset of HTML
2026-8-22
[html,compiler,superset,language]
This summer I was pretty bored, there was genuinely no fun project on my mind. Not that I ever planned on actually completing one, because for some reason finishing a project is really harder than starting a new one. There are always different kinds of ideas in my head, but when I sit down to work on them, I quickly lose motivation after about 2-3 weeks. I once made RIDBF, a Read-only Index-based DB Fetcher library with TypeScript, and to test it, I made a basic plain HTML/TS blog site with Bun.
<br/><br/>[\]
So then I came up with an idea of how I could easily integrate different languages on this blog website with libraries such as i18n, etc. Just as I started on that project, something struck me. I like plain HTML and TS. I think that is the peak form of writing frontend. I am strictly against non-reactive websites, such as news sites, using a heavy JS framework like React just to render some text and images. I like the speed and simplicity of good old plain HTML. (I think it is the way God intended for frontend .D). Jokes aside, I still like plain HTML, but I really dislike the need to copy and paste common UI elements like navbars.[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
With that idea in mind, I started working on a basic macro program in Rust which reads an HTML file as a raw string and replaces custom elements like <code>&lt;myElement/&gt;</code> with HTML written in an external file. After completing it, I realized that I wanted more than pure HTML.[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
I specifically wanted basic reactivity for this blog site. Because I use my RIDB package, every time a page loads, it first checks the browser's localStorage and then fetches from the hosted GitHub repo to see if a newer version is available. I needed a basic router for thisheavy operation to happen once, but I didn't want to use any runtime-heavy frameworks like React. I also really dislike the syntax and usage of JSX/TSX, so SolidJS was also a nogo. VueJS uses a VDOM, so that was immediately out too. (I hate it when basic text and image websites lag on my older laptops and phones just because they are using some "cool" VDOM framework with heavy animations). Angular seemed like the perfect choice at first, but it relies heavily on custom HTML syntax. I wanted pure HTML—something that would be loyal to idiomatic web standards. So I thought, "Why not make a superset of HTML? Like what TypeScript is to JavaScript, but for HTML, with just a tiny bit of runtime JS injected into it."[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
I wanted local JS blocks, I wanted scoped CSS, and things like that. Then I read about how to make a basic parser to parse HTML text into smaller chunks. With that, I made a very primitive lexer, then came an AST (Abstract Syntax Tree) parser, then I evaluated that into an eAST (Extended Abstract Syntax Tree) with hoisted variables, etc. After finishing that, I had a complete, working HTML parser which first tokenizes the text, parses it, and resolves it to pure data represented with Rust's smart enums and structs in RAM.[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
After completing that, I realized I could build more than just a basic component system. I added one custom tag, then another, then another. Before I knew it, I had built a very limited, largely primitive, tiny reactive superset of HTML.[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
<h2>How this HEML works.</h2>[\]
HEML is a superset of HTML. That means every valid HTML file is a compilable HEML file, just like how <code>tsc</code> works for TypeScript. HEML has a tiny minified core library called <code>core.min.js</code>. This library contains basic reactivity helper functions and one Observable class. This ~2kb of JS gets injected into every compiled HEML doc. Furthermore, `hemlc` also generates a small JS overhead at compile time, which also gets injected into the compiled HTML file. After walking through every imported component and their imported components (non-circular) recursively. It compiles one HEML doc into one HTML file.[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
<h2>What I like about it.</h2>[\]
This is the best tool (in my humble opinion), not because it is definitely the best, but because I designed it exactly how I see fit. It could probably be made better, but I built it however I pleased. I like simple, plain HTML syntax, so it gets simple HTML-like syntax. I like simple, basic, fast reactivity, so it gets that. Although I'm not sure whether a compiled HEML file would really outperform other compiled JS frameworks in terms of runtime performance (because I am lazy enough to not perform any benchmarks + not write any tests). There are problems (such as bridging between external JS sources & internal observables), and definitely BUGS, MANY BUGS. But this is not some serious tool suitable for production use, nor do I want it to be. I just wanted to make my life easier (+ happier) by building a tool I would love to use on my own personal projects.[\]
<br/><br/><br/><br/>[\]
BTW: I remade my personal blogsite with HEML. If you are curious about it, you can check the <a href='https://github.com/TannYuld/TannyuldBlog'>repo of this site</a>, or <a href='https://github.com/TannYuld/hemlc'>check the repo of hemlc</a> to learn more about it.[\]
